## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to architect provenance-aware prompt pipelines at scale, ensuring data lineage, watermarking, and auditability across tenants with realistic latency constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Data provenance and lineage in LLM prompts
- Per-tenant guards, watermarking, policy enforcement
- Output verification with source citations via a mock knowledge graph
- Structured audit logging: input hash, rewritten prompt, per-model outputs, latency, drift verdict
- Security considerations around prompt leakage and PII

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal prototype sketch
function rewriteWithProvenance(prompt, tenantPolicy) {
  const provenance = `Source=${tenantPolicy.allowedSources.join('|')};Tenant=${tenantPolicy.id}`;
  return `${prompt}\n\n[Provenance:${provenance}]`;
}
function verifyOutput(output, sources) {
  const hasCitation = sources.some(s => output.includes(s));
  return hasCitation;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle non-determinism across models while preserving provenance guarantees?
- How would you extend the audit log to support compliance reviews and exception handling?

## Why This Is Asked

Real-world fintech data assistants service multiple tenants; the router must enforce per-tenant policies, guard against data leakage, and handle conflicting signals and latency constraints.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant-aware policy retrieval and governance
- Guardrails and data-redaction
- Edge-case handling: conflicting signals, missing policy, leakage

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-router sketch
async function routePrompt(prompt, tenantId){
  const policy = await fetchPolicy(tenantId);
  if(!policy) throw new Error('Missing policy');
  if(!policy.allow(prompt)) throw new Error('Action not allowed');

  const intent = analyzeIntent(prompt);
  const module = selectModule(intent, policy);
  const plan = generatePlan(module, intent);

  return { module, plan, payload: buildPayload(module, prompt) };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Evaluates ability to design a simple, realistic prompt router with multilingual support, a common constraint in enterprise tooling at Citadel and Cloudflare.

## Key Concepts

- Language detection
- Intent routing
- Locale-aware payloads
- Edge-case handling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal router sketch (pseudo)
function route(prompt){
  const lang = detectLang(prompt);
  const intent = classify(prompt);
  const module = selectModule(intent, lang);
  return {module, payload: buildPayload(module, prompt, lang)};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test this with mixed-language prompts?
- How would latency constraints influence the design?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a robust, scalable prompt orchestration in a multilingual, latency-aware setting; integrates prompts, safety, fallback behavior, and observability.

## Key Concepts
- Intent classification for routing
- Policy-driven module selection
- Latency budgets and data sensitivity
- Cascading failures and fallback strategies
- Observability and auditability

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype
function routePrompt(prompt, budget) {
  const intent = classify(prompt);
  const module = policy.choose(intent, prompt.sensitivity, budget.latency);
  const payload = templates[module](prompt);
  return { module, payload };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test ambiguous intents?
- How would you handle dynamic policy updates at runtime?
